Local review patch: abort after a secondary-start acknowledgement timeout
Base: m1n1 4184923ffb2dff079b384d6a32cc02142aa14572, after 0001 through 0003
Provenance: AI-assisted project work; not submitted upstream.

A released CPU may enter late. Do not restore the shared reset pointers,
release another CPU using target_cpu, or return toward a kernel/HV handoff
when acknowledgement times out. Keep the published stacks and state intact
and enter the existing non-returning panic path.

This intentionally makes post-release timeout fatal on all supported SoCs.
Successful starts, pre-release skips, the void ABI, and the polling deadline
are unchanged. Panic may block flushing a console; reboot may remain in its
terminal loop if watchdog reset is unavailable. Neither constitutes proof
that a late core has stopped. This is control-flow containment, not native
reset validation, recoverable degraded SMP, or T6032 startup enablement.

--- a/src/smp.c
+++ b/src/smp.c
@@ -189,4 +189,5 @@
     if (i >= 100)
-        printf("Failed!\n");
+        /* A late CPU can still consume target_cpu and the shared reset stacks. */
+        panic("SMP: CPU %d start timed out; refusing to reuse reset state\n", index);
     else
         printf("  Started.\n");
